"reference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":164}); Total lines: 381\nL139: @a3nm Take a nice set $S$ whose area is $<0.01n^2$ and contains a length $n$ segment in every direction. Such a set $S$ exists by the constructions given on Wikipeida for the Kakeya set. Now pixelize $S$ by checking which unit squares it intersects from the unit grid. The number of these squares is $0.01n^2 +O(n)$ as the perimeter of $S$ is nice. As $n\\to \\infty$, this gives an upper bound of $0.01n^2$, and we can pick $0.01$ as small as we want to.\nL140: \nL141: –  【28†domotorp】\nL142: \nL143: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 8:53\nL144: \nL145:   * 1\nL146: I've asked about something similar about free polyominoes on 【29†Code Golf Stack Exchange†codegolf.stackexchange.com】 and 【30†Math Stack Exchange†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL147: \nL148: –  【31†Peter Kagey】\nL149: \nL150: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 2:11\nL151: \nL152:  |  【32†Show 17 more comments】\nL153: ## 3 Answers\nL154: \nL155: Sorted by:\nL156: \nL157: [Select]\nL158: \nL159: [Input]\nL160: \nL161: 9\nL162: \nL163: [Input]\nL164: It is actually $\\ge cn^2$ with some $c>0$. The value of $c$ I'll obtain is pretty dismal but I tried to trade the precision for the argument simplicity everywhere I could, so it can be certainly improved quite a bit. I have no doubt that it is written somewhere (perhaps, in the continuous form: the $2$-dimensional measure of a set containing a shift of every rectifiable curve of length $1$ is at least some positive constant) but I'll leave it to more educated people to provide the reference.\nL165: We shall work on the 2D $n\\times n$ lattice torus $T$ whose size $n$ is a power of $2$. Clearly, wrapping around makes the set only smaller. Define $K$ to be the integer such that $2^{K^3+K}\\le n< 2^{(K+1)^3+K+1}$ (I assume that $n$ is large enough, so $K$ is not too small either).\nL166: \nL167: Put $\\varepsilon_k=2^{-k}, M_k=2^{k^3}$ ($k\\ge 4$). Note that $\\frac 12+3\\sum_{k\\ge 4}\\varepsilon_k=\\frac 78<1$. Put $\\mu_k=\\frac 12+3\\sum_{m=4}^{k}\\varepsilon_m$ (so $\\mu_3=\\frac 12$).\nL168: Now take any set $E\\subset T$ of density $d(E,T)=\\frac{|E|}{|T|}=1/2$. Our aim will be to construct a connected set $P$ of cardinality $|P|\\le Cn$ such that no its lattice shift of $E$ on $T$ contains $P$.\nL169: Start with dividing $T$ into $M_4^2$ equal squares $Q_4$. Notice that the portion of squares $Q_4$ with density $d(E,Q_4)=\\frac{|E\\cap Q_4|}{|Q_4|}>\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $\\mu_3/(\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4)\\le 1-\\varepsilon_4$. Now choose $N_4=\\frac{2\\log_2 (M_4/\\varepsilon_4)}{\\varepsilon_4}=2\\cdot(4^3+4)\\cdot 2^4$ squares $Q_4$ independently at random.\nL170: The probability that none of them has density $d(E,Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $(1-\\varepsilon_4)^{N_4}< \\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon_4}{M_4}\\right)^2$. This means that if we consider not only the standard partition but also all its shifts $E'$ by multiples of $\\varepsilon_4 n/M_4$, then there exists a configuration $P_4$ of $N_4$ squares $Q_4$ such that for each such shift, the density of $E'$ in at least one square $Q_4$ in $P_4$ is $d(E',Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$.\nL171: However, every lattice shift can be approximated by such shifts with precision $\\varepsilon_4 n/M_4=\\varepsilon_4\\ell(Q_4)$, so we conclude that for any shift $E'$ of $E$, the configuration $P_4$ contains a square $Q_4$ with density $d(E',Q_4)\\le\\mu_3+3\\varepsilon_4=\\mu_4$.\nL172: Our $P$ will be essentially contained in $\\bigcup_{Q_4\\in P_4}Q_4$. Notice that we can construct some set in each square $Q_4$ and the cost of joining them afterwords will be at most $ 2n N_4 $. Notice also that the sidelength $\\ell(Q_4)$ of each $Q_4$ is $n/M_4$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":245}); Total lines: 381\nL240: Now suppose $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{Z}^2$ contains translates of all the polyominos of $P$ and $\\#A<\\frac{n^2}{10^{10}\\log(n)}$. Then, for some $a\\in A$ we have $\\#P_{A-a}\\geq\\frac{\\#P}{n^2}$. But on the other hand, $\\#(A-a)\\cap B\\leq\\#A<\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor$. Thus $P_{A-a}$ has $\\binom{\\#((A-a)\\cap B)}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}<\\binom{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}$ elements.\nL241: So $\\frac{\\#P_{A-a}}{\\#P}<\\frac{\\binom{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}}{\\binom{\\#B}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}} = \\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor\\left(\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-1\\right)\\dots\\left(\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor+1\\right)}{\\#B(\\#B-1)\\dots(\\#B-\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor+1))}$.\nL242: Moreover, as $\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor<\\#B$, for any $i=0,\\dots,\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor-1$ we have $\\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-i}{\\#B-i}\\leq\\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\#B}\\leq\\frac{1}{100}$\nL243: \nL244: So $\\frac{\\#P_{A-a}}{\\#P}\\leq\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)^{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}<\\frac{1}{n^2}$, a contradiction.\nL245: \nL246: Maybe a better choice of $P$ or other changes to this method could improve the bound on the asymptotic growth of $S_n$ a bit more.\nL247: \nL248: [Button: Cite]\nL249: \nL250: 【36†Improve this answer】\nL251: Follow [Input]\nL252: \nL253: 【37†edited May 6, 2022 at 18:43】\nL254: \nL255: answered Apr 26, 2022 at 22:45\nL256: \nL257: 【25†Saúl RM】\nL258: \nL259: 14.6k 2 2 gold badges 39 39 silver badges 64 64 bronze badges\nL260:   * Thanks a lot! Points that confused me: (i) \"two of them must be\" in the lemma is by the pigeonhole principle on squares of side sqrt(l). (ii) The lemma says that the polyomino contains all points $x_i, y_i$, but you use it to get polyominos $p_C$ whose intersection with the set $B$ is exactly some subset $C$. For this you need to ensure that the polyomino given by the lemma doesn't contain other points from $B$.\nL261: I guess this is doable without increasing the length too much: none of the neighbors of squares in $B$ are in $B$ by the \"even\" requirement so you can work around them.\nL262: –  【24†a3nm】\nL263: \nL264: Commented May 6, 2022 at 9:24\nL265:   * (iii) The \"Thus $P_{A-a}$\" is because, if a polyomino is in $P_{A-a}$, then its intersection with B is a subset of $(A-a) \\cap B$. (iv) unfortunately I didn't get the very last step; after the \"As when $n$ is big\" I don't see why this can derive the bound that follows and gives the contradiction? Other than that I was able to follow the argument at a high level (I didn't check the precise calculations), I think I got the idea.\nL266: This is very elegant and not at all the kind of techniques I would be used to. Thanks again. :)\nL267: –  【24†a3nm】\nL268: \nL269: Commented May 6, 2022 at 9:26\nL270: \nL271:   * You are welcome! I will edit the things you mention with a bit more detail.\nL272: \nL273: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL274: \nL275: Commented May 6, 2022 at 11:44\nL276: \nL277:   * 1\nL278: \nL279: Yeah my point is that you can consider the balls for Manhattan distance, which seems to make more intuitive sense given that the polynomino lengths follow the Manhattan distance. But I agree that this works no matter the distance up to changing the constants. Thanks a lot again for the insights!\nL280: \nL281: –  【24†a3nm】\nL282: Commented May 6, 2022 at 14:40\nL283: \nL284:   * 1\nL285: \nL286: You are right, I edited that. I wanted to use the \"normal\" distance but I guess if someone reaches that point of the proof they won't be scared of an $L1$ norm\nL287: \nL288: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL289: \nL290: Commented May 6, 2022 at 18:44\nL291: \nL292:  |  【32†Show 3 more comments】\nL293: \nL294: [Input]\nL295: \nL296: 4\nL297: \nL298: [Input]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":310}); Total lines: 2664\nL282:                             int weight = Enumerable.Range(0, width * height).Count(x => solver.Model[x] == Solver.l_True);\nL283: \nL284:                             Console.Write($\"{n}\\t<={weight}\\t{sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:F3}s\\t\");\nL285:                             int cell = 0;\nL286:                             for (int y = 0; y < height; y++)\nL287:                             {\nL288:                                 if (y > 0) Console.Write('_');\nL289:                                 for (int x = 0; x < width; x++) Console.Write(solver.Model[cell++] == Solver.l_True ? '#' : '.');\nL290:                             }\nL291:                             Console.WriteLine();\nL292: \nL293:                             // Now knock out that weight\nL294:                             for (int i = weight - 1; i < previous - 1; i++) solver.AddClause(~unaryWeights[i]);\nL295:                             previous = weight;\nL296:                         }\nL297:                         else\nL298:                         {\nL299:                             Console.WriteLine(\"--------\");\nL300:                             break;\nL301:                         }\nL302:                     }\nL303:                 }\nL304:             }\nL305: \nL306:             public static Tuple<Solver, Solver.Lit[]> Generate(IEnumerable<FreePolyomino> polys, int width, int height)\nL307:             {\nL308:                 var solver = new Solver();\nL309: \nL310:                 if (width == 12) solver.Prealloc(6037071 + 448, 72507588 + 6008); // HACK!\nL311:                 // Variables: 0 to width * height - 1 are the cells available to fill.\nL312:                 for (int i = 0; i < width * height; i++) solver.NewVar();\nL313: \nL314:                 foreach (var poly in polys)\nL315:                 {\nL316:                     // We naturally get a DNF: each position of each orientation is a conjunction of poly.Weight variables,\nL317:                     // and we require any one. Therefore we add an auxiliary variable per.\nL318: \nL319:                     var polyAuxs = new List<Solver.Lit>();\nL320:                     foreach (var orientation in poly.OrientedPolyominos)\nL321:                     {\nL322:                         int maxh = height;\nL323:                         // Optimisation: break symmetry\nL324:                         if (orientation.BBHeight == 1) maxh = ((height + 1) >> 1);\nL325: \nL326:                         for (int dy = 0; dy + orientation.BBHeight <= maxh; dy++)\nL327:                         {\nL328:                             for (int dx = 0; dx + orientation.BBWidth <= width; dx++)\nL329:                             {\nL330:                                 var currentAux = solver.NewVar();\nL331:                                 for (int y = 0; y < orientation.BBHeight; y++)\nL332:                                 {\nL333:                                     uint tmp = orientation.Rows[y];\nL334:                                     for (int x = 0; tmp > 0; x++, tmp >>= 1)\nL335:                                     {\nL336:                                         if ((tmp & 1) == 1) solver.AddClause(~currentAux, new Solver.Lit((y + dy) * width + x + dx));\nL337:                                     }\nL338:                                 }\nL339: \nL340:                                 polyAuxs.Add(currentAux);\nL341:                             }\nL342:                         }\nL343:                     }\nL344:                     solver.AddClause(polyAuxs.ToArray());\nL345:                 }\nL346: \nL347:                 // Efficient CNF encoding of Boolean cardinality constraints, Bailleux and Boufkhad, http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.458.7676&rep=rep1&type=pdf\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"transpose\"}); Total lines: 2664\nL2422:         fn flip_hor(&self) -> Self {\nL2423:             Poly::new(self.0.iter().map(|&(a, b)| (-a, b)).collect())\nL2424:         }\nL2425:         fn flip_vert(&self) -> Self {\nL2426:             Poly::new(self.0.iter().map(|&(a, b)| (a, -b)).collect())\nL2427:         }\nL2428:         fn transpose(&self) -> Self {\nL2429:             Poly::new(self.0.iter().map(|&(a, b)| (b, a)).collect())\nL2430:         }\nL2431:         fn offset_by(&self, x: isize, y: isize) -> Self {\nL2432:             Poly::new(self.0.iter().map(|&(a, b)| (a+x, b+y)).collect())\nL2433:         }\nL2434:         fn offset_canon(&self) -> Self {\nL2435:             let (mut min_x, mut min_y) = self.0[0];\nL2436:             for &(x, y) in &self.0 {\nL2437:                 if x < min_x {\nL2438:                     min_x = x;\nL2439:                 }\nL2440:                 if y < min_y {\nL2441:                     min_y = y;\nL2442:                 }\nL2443:             }\nL2444:             self.offset_by(-min_x, -min_y)\nL2445:         }\nL2446:         fn transformations(&self) -> Vec<Self> {\nL2447:             vec!(\nL2448:                 self.offset_canon(),\nL2449:                 self.flip_hor().offset_canon(),\nL2450:                 self.flip_vert().offset_canon(),\nL2451:                 self.flip_vert().flip_hor().offset_canon(),\nL2452:                 self.transpose().offset_canon(),\nL2453:                 self.transpose().flip_hor().offset_canon(),\nL2454:                 self.transpose().flip_vert().offset_canon(),\nL2455:                 self.transpose().flip_vert().flip_hor().offset_canon(),\nL2456:             )\nL2457:         }\nL2458:         fn canonicalize(&self) -> Self {\nL2459:             self.transformations().into_iter().min().unwrap().transpose()\nL2460:         }\nL2461:         fn max_box(&self) -> (isize, isize) {\nL2462:             let (mut max_x, mut max_y) = self.0[0];\nL2463:             for &(x, y) in &self.0 {\nL2464:                 if x > max_x {\nL2465:                     max_x = x;\nL2466:                 }\nL2467:                 if y > max_y {\nL2468:                     max_y = y;\nL2469:                 }\nL2470:             }\nL2471:             (max_x, max_y)\nL2472:         }\nL2473:         fn extend(&self) -> HashSet<Self> {\nL2474:             let elems: HashSet<(isize, isize)> = self.0.iter().cloned().collect();\nL2475:             let mut perim: HashSet<(isize, isize)> = HashSet::new();\nL2476:             let mut neighbors: HashSet<Self> = HashSet::new();\nL2477:             for &(x, y) in &self.0 {\nL2478:                 for (dx, dy) in vec!((0, 1), (1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, -1)) {\nL2479:                     let p = (x + dx, y + dy);\nL2480:                     if !elems.contains(&p) {\nL2481:                         if !perim.contains(&p) {\nL2482:                             let mut poly_points = self.0.clone();\nL2483:                             poly_points.push(p);\nL2484:                             let new_poly = Poly::new(poly_points).canonicalize();\nL2485:                             neighbors.insert(new_poly);\nL2486:                             perim.insert(p);\nL2487:                         }\nL2488:                     }\nL2489:                 }\nL2490:             }\nL2491:             neighbors\nL2492:         }\nL2493:     }\nL2494: \nL2495:     impl fmt::Display for Poly {\nL2496:         fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\nL2497:             let p = self.offset_canon();\nL2498:             let (max_x, max_y) = p.max_box();\nL2499:             let (max_x, max_y) = (max_x as usize, max_y as usize);\nL2500:             let mut grid = vec![vec![\" \"; max_x+1]; max_y+1];\nL2501:             for &(x, y) in &p.0 {\nL2502:                 grid[y as usize][x as usize] = \"#\"\nL2503:             }\nL2504:             let s = grid.into_iter().map(|r| r.concat()).collect::<Vec<String>>().join(\"\\n\");\nL2505:             write!(f, \"{}\", s)\nL2506:         }\nL2507:     }\nL2508: \nL2509:     fn all_polys(n: usize) -> HashSet<Poly> {\nL2510:         let mut polys = HashSet::new();\nL2511:         polys.insert(Poly::new(vec!((0, 0))));\nL2512:         for _ in 0..n-1 {\nL2513:             let mut next_polys = HashSet::new();\nL2514:             for poly in polys {\nL2515:                 next_polys.extend(poly.extend());\nL2516:             }\nL2517:             polys = next_polys;\nL2518:         }\nL2519:         polys\nL2520:     }\nL2521: \nL2522:     fn overlap_polys(polys: &Vec<Poly>, seed: u64) -> Poly {\nL2523:         let mut seq = polys.clone();\nL2524:         let mut seed_array = [0; 32];\nL2525:         for i in 0..32 {\nL2526:             seed_array[i] = seed.to_be().to_bytes()[i%8];\nL2527:         }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"Orientations\"}); Total lines: 2664\nL368:                             if (alpha > 0 && beta > 0) solver.AddClause(~left[alpha - 1], ~right[beta - 1], sum[sigma - 1]);\nL369:                             else if (alpha > 0) solver.AddClause(~left[alpha - 1], sum[sigma - 1]);\nL370:                             else if (beta > 0) solver.AddClause(~right[beta - 1], sum[sigma - 1]);\nL371: \nL372:                             // C_2 = left[alpha] + right[beta] + ~sum[sigma]\nL373:                             if (alpha < left.Length && beta < right.Length) solver.AddClause(left[alpha], right[beta], ~sum[sigma]);\nL374:                             else if (alpha < left.Length) solver.AddClause(left[alpha], ~sum[sigma]);\nL375:                             else if (beta < right.Length) solver.AddClause(right[beta], ~sum[sigma]);\nL376:                         }\nL377:                     }\nL378:                 }\nL379: \nL380:                 return sum;\nL381:             }\nL382:         }\nL383:         class FreePolyomino : IEquatable<FreePolyomino>\nL384:         {\nL385:             internal FreePolyomino(OrientedPolyomino orientation)\nL386:             {\nL387:                 var orientations = new HashSet<OrientedPolyomino>();\nL388:                 orientations.Add(orientation);\nL389:                 var tmp = orientation.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL390:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL391:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL392:                 tmp = tmp.FlipV(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL393:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL